<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/20th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty</p><h1 class="enc-title">Japan–Korea Treaty of 1905</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1905">1905</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Eulsa Treaty, Eulsa Protectorate Treaty</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> Treaty of 1905, known in Korea as the Eulsa Treaty, was signed on 17 November 1905 after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-japanese-war">Russo-Japanese War</a>. Pushed through by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Itō Hirobumi</a> with troops surrounding the palace and without the emperor's signature, it took control of Korea's foreign relations and made the country a Japanese <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/protectorate">protectorate</a> under a resident-general. Korea's emperor Gojong denounced it as invalid, and Japan annexed Korea in 1910.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/japan-korea-treaty-of-1905.jpg" alt="Japan–Korea Treaty of 1905" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: RYU Cheol · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jeongdong19.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The treaty followed earlier agreements that had narrowed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a>'s independence. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>–Korea Protocol of 23 February 1904 let Japan occupy strategically important places, and the First Japan–Korea Agreement of August 1904 required Korea to accept financial and diplomatic advisers sent by Japan. After the victory over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>, a Japanese cabinet meeting on 27 October 1905 agreed eight provisions for a second treaty giving Japan absolute authority over Korea's foreign affairs.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ito-hirobumi">Itō Hirobumi</a> arrived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> on 9 November 1905. Gojong first refused to meet him, citing illness, and gave way on 15 November when Itō ordered Japanese troops to encircle the palace. He refused to order his foreign minister to negotiate, arguing that such matters needed the approval of the Korean Privy Council.</p><p>On 17 November the Korean ministers refused to sign at the Japanese legation and again at an <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-conference">Imperial Conference</a> at Gyeongungung palace. That evening Itō and Field Marshal Hasegawa Yoshimichi came with military police and questioned each minister, with the implied threat of bodily harm. Han Kyu-seol and Min Yeong-gi objected openly, and with the approval of five of the eight present Itō declared the agreement valid. The Foreign Minister's seal was affixed about 1:30 in the morning of 18 November.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Terms</h2><p>The treaty had five articles. It forbade the Korean government to sign any treaty of an international nature without Japanese supervision. Under Article 3 a Resident-General was to be sent to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> to handle diplomatic affairs, with the right to visit the Korean emperor privately. It was to remain in force until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> became wealthy and strong, a clause proposed by Yi Wan-yong.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> closed its legations abroad, among them those in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/beijing">Beijing</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/washington-dc">Washington, D.C.</a>, and foreign legations in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/seoul">Seoul</a> began to withdraw, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> being the last. The Residency-General was established in February 1906 with Itō as first Resident-General. Gojong sought help from other countries: a telegram to Homer Hulbert in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> and a secret letter smuggled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> by the reporter Douglas Story brought no result. In 1907 he sent three emissaries to the Second Hague Peace Conference, who were barred from the conference; he was then forced to abdicate, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a>–Korea Treaty of 1907 of 24 July transferred authority over Korea's internal affairs to the Resident-General.</p><p>Min Yeong-hwan and Cho Byeong-se killed themselves in protest, and local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yangban">yangbans</a> and commoners formed righteous armies. In <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/korea">Korea</a> the five ministers who approved the treaty are widely called the Five Eulsa Traitors. The 1965 Treaty on Basic Relations confirmed the treaty as already null and void, but its legality in 1905 remains disputed. Korean scholars argue that it was never valid, being negotiated under threat of force without Gojong's approval, while several Japanese studies conclude that he consented, evidence Korean academics question.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Japan–Korea Treaty of 1905” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>By the <b>Japan–Korea Treaty of 1905</b> Korea became a protectorate ruled through the Resident-General, and the Korean Army was cut from 20,000 to 1,000 men.</blockquote><a href="/empire/korea-under-japanese-rule">Korea under Japanese rule · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The <b>Eulsa Treaty</b> of 1905 made Korea a Japanese protectorate under Resident-General Ito Hirobumi.</blockquote><a href="/state/korean-empire">Korean Empire</a></li></ul></section>
